CAT = "polReading"; SEG = "RDG"
# Each item: (stem with its own passage, key, [3 wrong], explanation). Passages are original and
# describe a fictional department's procedures; they test reading, not any agency's real policy.
Q = [
("Read the passage: Officers must complete a use-of-force report before the end of the shift in which force was used. The report goes to the shift supervisor, who reviews it within 72 hours and forwards it to the training unit. If the supervisor finds that the force may have violated policy, the report goes to internal affairs instead of the training unit. According to the passage, when does a report go to internal affairs?",
 "When force may have broken policy", ["When the supervisor misses 72 hours", "When the officer adds a supplement", "When the training unit requests it"],
 "The passage sends a report to internal affairs only when the supervisor finds the force may have violated policy. The 72-hour review time and supplements have nothing to do with where the report goes."),
("Read the passage: Property and evidence must be packaged separately. Each item gets its own bag or container, sealed with evidence tape, and the sealing officer writes initials and the date across the tape. Firearms are unloaded before packaging. Wet items, such as bloody clothing, are air-dried before they are sealed, because sealing them wet can destroy biological evidence. Why does the passage say wet items are dried before sealing?",
 "To protect biological evidence", ["To make the tape stick properly", "To reduce the weight of the bag", "To keep the items from smelling"],
 "The passage gives one reason: sealing wet items can destroy biological evidence. The other options may sound sensible but are not stated."),
("Read the passage: When officers arrive at a disturbance call, one officer speaks with each party in a separate area, out of sight and hearing of the other. Separating the parties lets each person speak freely. Officers then compare the two accounts with what any witnesses saw and with the physical evidence before deciding what happened. Which reason for separating the parties does the passage give?",
 "So each person can speak freely", ["So the scene can be photographed", "So each person can be searched", "So the officers can finish faster"],
 "The passage says separation lets each person speak freely. Photographs, searches and speed are not mentioned."),
("Read the passage: A vehicle pursuit may be started only when the officer has reason to believe the driver has committed a violent felony. The officer must end the pursuit if the risk to the public becomes greater than the need to catch the suspect, or when a supervisor orders it. Pursuits for traffic violations, stolen property or misdemeanors are not allowed under this policy. Under this policy, which situation allows a pursuit to begin?",
 "A driver who just shot someone", ["A driver who ran a red light", "A driver who stole a car", "A driver with a suspended license"],
 "Only a violent felony allows a pursuit, and a shooting is one. The passage rules out traffic violations and stolen property."),
("Read the passage: There is no waiting period before a missing person report is taken. Officers take the report as soon as it is made, whether the person has been gone for one hour or one week. Reports involving children under 13, people with dementia, or anyone who may be in danger are treated as high-risk and are broadcast to all units at once. Which case does the passage treat as high-risk?",
 "A man with dementia missing an hour", ["A 30-year-old missing two days", "A 17-year-old who skipped school", "A 40-year-old who missed work"],
 "People with dementia are high-risk no matter how long they have been gone. Time missing alone does not make a case high-risk under the passage."),
("Read the passage: At 2:10 a.m. Officer Reyes found the rear door of a hardware store open. The lock was undamaged and nothing appeared to be missing. The owner said by phone that the last employee to leave had closed at 9 p.m. and was new to the job. The owner came to the store, checked the stock and the cash drawer, and locked the door. Which conclusion is best supported by the passage?",
 "The door was likely left unlocked", ["A burglar forced the door open", "Cash was taken from the drawer", "The new employee stole stock"],
 "An undamaged lock, nothing missing and a new employee closing up all point to a door left unlocked. Nothing supports forced entry or theft."),
("Read the passage: Officers must turn on their body-worn camera before arriving at any call for service and keep it recording until they leave the scene. An officer may stop recording while speaking with a victim of sexual assault who asks not to be recorded, but must state the reason aloud before turning the camera off. Failing to record is reported to the officer's supervisor. Before turning off the camera for a victim who asks, the officer must:",
 "State the reason on camera", ["Get a supervisor's approval", "Have the victim sign a form", "Finish the whole interview"],
 "The passage requires the officer to state the reason aloud before turning the camera off. Supervisor approval and a signed form are not required."),
("Read the passage: Most residential burglaries in the city happen between 10 a.m. and 3 p.m. on weekdays, when homes are most likely to be empty. Burglars usually enter through an unlocked door or window rather than by force. Because of this, the crime prevention unit spends most of its effort urging residents to lock doors and windows, even when they leave for a short time. Why does the unit focus on getting residents to lock up?",
 "Most burglars enter unlocked homes", ["Most burglaries happen at night", "Locks are cheaper than alarms", "Forced entry is the most common method"],
 "The passage links the lock-up message to the fact that burglars usually walk in through unlocked doors and windows. It says burglaries happen mostly in the daytime, not at night."),
("Read the passage: During a traffic stop at night, officers park the patrol car about one car length behind the stopped vehicle and slightly to the left, so the patrol car shields the officer from passing traffic. Spotlights are aimed at the stopped car's mirrors, which makes it hard for the driver to see the officer walk up. According to the passage, the patrol car is offset to the left to:",
 "Shield the officer from traffic", ["Light up the driver's mirrors", "Block the car from driving off", "Give the driver room to exit"],
 "The offset is there so the patrol car shields the officer from passing traffic. Lighting the mirrors is the job of the spotlights, not the offset."),
("Read the passage: Juveniles taken into custody are kept out of sight and sound of adult prisoners at all times. A juvenile may be held in a secure room for no more than six hours while officers find a parent or guardian. If no parent can be found in that time, the officer contacts the on-call juvenile probation officer for instructions. What does the officer do if no parent is found within six hours?",
 "Calls the on-call probation officer", ["Moves the youth to the adult cells", "Releases the youth to a neighbor", "Holds the youth for another six hours"],
 "The passage says to contact the on-call juvenile probation officer. Holding longer than six hours or mixing with adults breaks the stated rules."),
("Read the passage: Officers should use plain language in reports. Words such as \"proceeded\" and \"observed\" can be replaced with \"went\" and \"saw.\" Short sentences in the order events happened are easier for prosecutors and juries to follow. Abbreviations should be spelled out the first time they are used. Which change does the passage recommend?",
 "Using went instead of proceeded", ["Writing events in order of importance", "Using longer, more formal sentences", "Never spelling out abbreviations"],
 "The passage gives went for proceeded as an example of plain language. It asks for time order, short sentences and spelling out abbreviations the first time."),
("Read the passage: Latent prints are invisible prints left by oils and sweat on a surface. Smooth, non-porous surfaces such as glass and painted metal are usually dusted with powder. Porous surfaces such as paper and cardboard absorb the oils, so prints on them are developed with chemicals in the lab instead of powder at the scene. According to the passage, how should prints on a cardboard box be handled?",
 "Developed with lab chemicals", ["Dusted with powder at the scene", "Lifted with tape at the scene", "Dusted after the box dries out"],
 "Cardboard is porous, and the passage says porous items are developed with chemicals in the lab. Powder is for smooth surfaces like glass."),
("Read the passage: During the parade, officers on foot are assigned to fixed posts along the route and must not leave them except to handle an emergency at or near the post. Officers who need a break notify the sector sergeant, who sends a relief officer. Lost children are taken to the first-aid tent at the end of the route, where parents are told to check. According to the passage, where does a lost child go?",
 "The first-aid tent", ["The sector sergeant", "The nearest patrol car", "The parade start point"],
 "The passage says lost children go to the first-aid tent at the end of the route. The sector sergeant handles relief for breaks."),
("Read the passage: Before a vehicle is entered as stolen in the state system, the officer confirms with the owner that the car was not repossessed, towed or borrowed by a family member. Many vehicles reported stolen turn out to have been towed. Entering a vehicle that is not stolen can lead to an innocent driver being stopped at gunpoint. Why does the officer check for a tow or repossession first?",
 "To avoid stops of innocent drivers", ["To find the vehicle more quickly", "To charge the owner a towing fee", "To save the owner an insurance claim"],
 "The passage warns that a wrong stolen entry can get an innocent driver stopped at gunpoint. Fees and insurance are not mentioned."),
("Read the passage: When a person appears to be in a mental health crisis, officers slow the encounter down when it is safe to do so. They give the person space, speak calmly, use the person's name and avoid giving several commands at once. A crisis team clinician can be asked to respond with officers during daytime hours. Which approach fits the passage?",
 "Calm speech, one request at a time", ["Giving several fast commands at once", "Standing close to keep control", "Waiting for a clinician at night"],
 "The passage asks for calm speech and warns against several commands at once. It says to give space, and clinicians are available in the daytime."),
("Read the passage: In one county, a search warrant must be served within ten days of being issued. After the search, the officer leaves a copy of the warrant and a receipt for any property taken at the place searched. The officer then returns the warrant to the court with an inventory of the property. What must the officer leave at the place searched?",
 "A warrant copy and a receipt", ["The inventory sent to the court", "A list of officers who searched", "A business card and phone number"],
 "The passage says a copy of the warrant and a receipt are left at the place searched. The inventory goes back to the court."),
("Read the passage: Officers may not work more than 16 hours in any 24-hour period, counting regular shifts, overtime and off-duty jobs. After working 16 hours, an officer must have at least 8 hours off before returning to duty. Supervisors check hours before approving any overtime. An officer has worked a 12-hour shift. How many more hours may she work in that 24-hour period?",
 "4 hours", ["8 hours", "6 hours", "12 hours"],
 "The limit is 16 hours in 24, and 16 minus 12 is 4. The 8 hours in the passage is the minimum rest period, not the hours left to work."),
("Read the passage: When showing a photo lineup, the officer who presents the photos should not know which photo is the suspect. The witness is told that the person may or may not be in the lineup and that the investigation will continue either way. The witness's confidence is written down in the witness's own words right after any identification. Why should the presenting officer not know which photo is the suspect?",
 "So no cues are given to the witness", ["So the lineup can be done faster", "So the officer can testify later", "So the witness feels more confident"],
 "An officer who does not know the suspect cannot give hints, even by accident. That is the purpose of a blind lineup; speed and confidence are not the reason."),
("Read the passage: Radio traffic should be brief. Officers wait for a clear channel, identify themselves by unit number and give their location first, because if the transmission is cut off, the location is the most important detail. Emergency traffic takes priority over all other transmissions. According to the passage, why is the location given first?",
 "It matters most if the call drops", ["It is the shortest part to say", "It identifies the officer's unit", "It clears the channel for others"],
 "The passage says location goes first because it is the most important detail if the transmission is cut off. The unit number is what identifies the officer."),
("Read the passage: Bicycle patrol officers cover the downtown area from 10 a.m. to 8 p.m. In bad weather, they switch to foot patrol rather than return to the station. Bicycle officers are especially useful in crowds and parks, where patrol cars cannot easily go, and they can often approach quietly. What do bicycle officers do in bad weather?",
 "Switch to foot patrol", ["Return to the station", "Switch to patrol cars", "Patrol only the parks"],
 "The passage says they switch to foot patrol rather than return to the station. Patrol cars are not mentioned as a bad-weather option."),
("Read the passage: At a collision scene, the first duty is to protect the scene and the people in it: park to block traffic, turn on emergency lights and check for injuries. Only after the injured are cared for and the scene is safe do officers take measurements and statements. Vehicles are moved only when they block traffic and only after photographs of their positions have been taken. Before vehicles are moved, the passage says officers must:",
 "Photograph where they came to rest", ["Get written statements from both drivers", "Measure all skid marks first", "Wait for the tow trucks to arrive"],
 "The passage allows moving vehicles only after their positions are photographed. Statements and measurements come later and are not a condition for moving them."),
("Read the passage: Officers in a foot pursuit should broadcast their direction of travel and a description of the suspect. Officers should not split up from a partner, follow a suspect into a building alone, or keep going once they have lost sight of the suspect. In those cases they set up a perimeter and wait for help. A suspect runs into a warehouse and a lone officer loses sight of him. The passage says the officer should:",
 "Set up a perimeter and wait", ["Search the warehouse alone", "Return to the patrol car", "Keep running the same direction"],
 "Losing sight of the suspect and facing a building alone are both cases where the passage says to set up a perimeter and wait for help."),
("Read the passage: Patrol officers carry naloxone nasal spray. If a person is unresponsive and an opioid overdose is suspected, the officer calls for medical help, gives one dose and starts rescue breathing or CPR as trained. If there is no response after two to three minutes, a second dose may be given. The person must be watched closely, since naloxone can wear off before the opioid does. Why does the passage say the person must be watched after naloxone?",
 "It may wear off before the opioid", ["It can cause a second overdose", "It must be given every minute", "It works only after CPR starts"],
 "The passage says naloxone can wear off before the opioid does, so breathing can stop again. The second dose is given after two to three minutes, not every minute."),
("Read the passage: Reports written soon after an incident are more accurate than reports written days later. Memory fades quickly, and details such as times, distances and exact words spoken are the first to be lost. For this reason, officers take brief notes at the scene and write the full report before the end of the shift. What is the main idea of the passage?",
 "Write reports while memory is fresh", ["Notes can replace a full report", "Exact quotes belong only in notes", "Distances must be measured twice"],
 "Every sentence supports writing the report soon, before memory fades. Notes are a help, not a replacement for the report."),
("Read the passage: A police dog may be used to track a suspect who has fled the scene of a felony. Before sending the dog into a building, the handler gives a loud warning, waits a reasonable time for the suspect to surrender and repeats the warning. Other officers stay behind the handler during a track so they do not lay down their own scent. Why do other officers stay behind the handler?",
 "To avoid adding their own scent", ["To protect the dog from the suspect", "To hear the handler's warnings", "To keep the dog from turning back"],
 "The passage says officers stay behind so they do not lay down their own scent, which could confuse the dog."),
("Read the passage: When a victim or witness speaks limited English, officers use a qualified interpreter, in person or by phone. Family members, and especially children, should not interpret except in an emergency, because they may change or soften what is said and may themselves be affected by the case. Why does the passage advise against using children as interpreters?",
 "They may change what is said", ["They cannot be paid for it", "Phone lines do not allow it", "They must be in school then"],
 "The passage says family members, especially children, may change or soften what is said. Pay and school hours are not mentioned."),
("Read the passage: Every person who handles an item of evidence signs the chain-of-custody form with the date, the time and the reason for handling it. A gap in the chain does not automatically keep evidence out of court, but it gives the defense a reason to question whether the item was changed. According to the passage, a gap in the chain of custody:",
 "Lets the defense question the item", ["Always keeps the item out of court", "Means the item must be retested", "Must be fixed by the prosecutor"],
 "The passage says a gap does not automatically exclude the evidence, but it lets the defense question whether the item was changed."),
("Read the passage: In the beat officer program, each officer is assigned to the same neighborhood for at least two years. Officers attend community meetings, learn the names of business owners and work with residents to solve repeat problems such as noise or abandoned cars. The goal is to prevent crime by fixing the conditions that lead to it, not only to respond after it happens. What is the goal of the program described?",
 "Preventing crime by fixing its causes", ["Responding faster to emergency calls", "Rotating officers to new areas", "Increasing arrests for noise complaints"],
 "The last sentence states the goal: prevent crime by fixing the conditions that lead to it. Officers stay in one area, so rotation is wrong."),
("Read the passage: If an officer arrives at a crash involving a truck with hazardous-materials placards, the officer stays upwind and uphill, at a distance, and reads the placard number with binoculars if possible. The officer must not walk into a vapor cloud or spill to rescue someone without protective equipment, but should direct people who can walk to move away. Where should the officer stay at the scene?",
 "Upwind and uphill, at a distance", ["Downwind, close enough to read", "Beside the truck to block traffic", "Downhill, near the spill's edge"],
 "The passage says upwind, uphill and at a distance, reading the placard with binoculars. Downwind and downhill are where vapors and liquids travel."),
("Read the passage: Residents 18 and older may apply to ride with an officer for one shift. Applicants sign a waiver and pass a background check. Riders must stay in the car during high-risk calls and follow the officer's directions at all times. A person may ride no more than twice in a calendar year. A rider went out in March and in June. Can she ride again in October of the same year?",
 "No, the yearly limit is reached", ["Yes, if she passes another check", "Yes, if a new waiver is signed", "No, riders may go only once"],
 "The limit is two rides per calendar year, and she has used both. The limit is two, not one."),
("Read the passage: Open-ended questions, such as asking a witness to describe what happened, let witnesses tell events in their own words and usually produce more information. Leading questions, which suggest an answer, can change what a witness remembers. Closed questions, which call for a short answer, are best saved for checking details at the end. Which question is best for opening a witness interview?",
 "Tell me everything you saw.", ["Was the car a red sedan?", "Did he have a gun in his hand?", "The man ran north, didn't he?"],
 "An open request lets the witness tell the story in their own words. The others are closed or leading questions that the passage saves for later or warns against."),
("Read the passage: Store security staff may detain a shoplifting suspect and call police. When officers arrive, they get a written statement from the employee who saw the theft, collect any video, and photograph the property before it is returned to the store. The merchandise does not need to be kept as evidence once it is photographed, unless it is damaged. When must the merchandise be kept as evidence?",
 "When it has been damaged", ["When there is no video of it", "When the suspect denies it", "When security staff ask for it"],
 "The passage keeps merchandise as evidence only when it is damaged. Otherwise a photograph is enough."),
("Read the passage: Officer Lin noted that the suspect's car hood was warm at 11:40 p.m., although the suspect said he had been home since 8 p.m. The night temperature was 40 degrees, and the car was parked outdoors in the driveway. What can best be inferred?",
 "The car was driven recently", ["The suspect was home at 8 p.m.", "The hood warms in cold weather", "The car was parked in a garage"],
 "A hood that is still warm on a 40-degree night points to recent driving, which conflicts with the suspect's story. The car was outdoors, not in a garage."),
("Read the passage: For this policy, a critical incident is any event in which a person is seriously injured or killed as a result of police action, or in which an officer fires a weapon at a person, whether or not anyone is hit. After a critical incident, the officers involved are separated and give a full statement only after a rest period. Which event is a critical incident under this definition?",
 "An officer fires at a person and misses", ["An officer's car is hit while parked", "A suspect is bruised while handcuffed", "An officer fires at a dangerous dog"],
 "Firing at a person counts whether or not anyone is hit. A bruise is not a serious injury, and the definition covers shots at people, not animals."),
("Read the passage: Patrol shifts run in three watches: day watch from 6 a.m. to 4 p.m., swing watch from 2 p.m. to midnight, and night watch from 10 p.m. to 8 a.m. The overlaps allow briefings and keep extra officers on the street during the busiest hours. At 3 p.m., which watches are on duty?",
 "Day and swing watch", ["Swing and night watch", "Day watch only", "Night and day watch"],
 "Day watch runs until 4 p.m. and swing watch starts at 2 p.m., so both are on at 3 p.m. Night watch does not start until 10 p.m."),
("Read the passage: Reports should record what the officer saw, heard and did, not the officer's opinions. Instead of writing that a driver was drunk, the officer should describe the signs: the odor of alcohol, slurred speech, unsteady balance, and red, watery eyes. The facts allow the reader to reach the conclusion. Which sentence follows the passage's advice?",
 "He swayed and his speech was slurred.", ["He was obviously very drunk.", "He seemed like a heavy drinker.", "He was clearly far too drunk to drive."],
 "Swaying and slurred speech are observed signs. The other sentences state conclusions or opinions, which the passage says to avoid."),
("Read the passage: Animal control handles stray, injured and dead animals during business hours. After hours, patrol officers respond only to animals that are an immediate danger to people, such as a dog attacking someone, or to an animal hit by a car that is blocking a roadway. Other animal calls are logged for animal control the next morning. At 11 p.m. a caller reports a stray cat in her yard. What happens?",
 "It is logged for the morning", ["A patrol officer responds", "Animal control responds now", "The caller is told to trap it"],
 "A stray cat in a yard is not an immediate danger or a road hazard, so the call is logged for animal control the next morning."),
("Read the passage: Every year many officers die in traffic crashes. Wearing a seat belt, slowing down when responding to calls and staying alert at roadside stops are simple steps that save lives. The department's goal this year is full seat belt use on every shift. What is the main purpose of the passage?",
 "To urge safe driving habits", ["To report this year's crash data", "To explain roadside stop law", "To compare types of incidents"],
 "The passage lists safe habits and sets a seat belt goal, so its purpose is to persuade officers to drive safely. It gives no crash figures or laws."),
("Read the passage: Graffiti should be photographed and reported before it is removed. Repeated tags by the same writer can link many incidents to one suspect, which raises the total damage and can change how the case is charged. Property owners are asked to remove graffiti within 48 hours, since quick removal discourages new tagging. Why should graffiti be photographed before removal?",
 "To link tags to one suspect", ["To prove the owner removed it", "To show it was removed in 48 hours", "To bill the owner for cleanup"],
 "Photos let investigators connect repeated tags to one writer, which matters for the charge. The 48-hour removal is about discouraging new tags."),
("Read the passage: During a building search, officers move slowly, clear one room at a time and mark rooms that have been cleared. They check places a person could hide, such as closets, crawl spaces and under beds, and never pass an uncleared doorway with their back to it. According to the passage, officers searching a building should:",
 "Clear one room at a time", ["Clear the largest rooms first", "Search as fast as possible", "Skip spaces too small to stand in"],
 "The passage says to move slowly and clear one room at a time, including small hiding places such as crawl spaces."),
]
